{"author":{"id":"29588b87a98184856c6eb9104921c34cca98a9cb029a2ee86cc10622d693ba86"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794880129,"nonce":"6CjS3DTTfjIca5zLHhnq+N+9lPA=","title":"F1-F9, F11, F12 and Insert give key code 0 in the windowed event loop, so no shortcut can use them","message":"Version: Blinc at cf925af (tag fs-blinc-3.1); the same table is on project-blinc/Blinc main (67f54d2).\n\nThe desktop platform layer converts the F keys and Insert correctly. The window then reduces the key to a numeric code with a fixed table, and that table has no entry for them, so the code is 0:\n\n- extensions/blinc_platform_desktop/src/input.rs:52 (Insert) and :71-82 (F1-F12) turn the winit named keys into Key::Insert and Key::F1..Key::F12 (same lines on cf925af and on main).\n- crates/blinc_app/src/windowed/event_loop/input/keyboard.rs, `let key_code = match \u0026kb_event.key` (:143 on cf925af, :137 on main) lists Key::F10 =\u003e 121 (:148 / :142) but no other F key and no Insert; the match ends in `_ =\u003e 0` (:225 on cf925af, :219 on main).\n- Every KEY_DOWN is pushed with that code (keyboard.rs:239 on cf925af, :233 on main: router.on_key_down(key_code)). The event carries no Key value, only the code, so a handler cannot tell F5 from any other unmapped key.\n\nEffect: an on_key_down handler sees ctx.key_code == 0 for F1, F2, F5 and the other unmapped F keys and for Insert. A menu or shortcut table cannot bind them (F1 for help, F2 for rename and F5 for reload are common). Keys that are mapped today: letters, digits, punctuation, arrows, Home/End/Page, Delete, Backspace, Tab, Enter, Escape, Space, F10 and the menu key.\n\nMinimal reproduction: in any windowed app put `.on_key_down(|ctx| println!(\"{}\", ctx.key_code))` on the root element and press F5, then F10. F5 prints 0, F10 prints 121.\n\nExpected: F1-F12 and Insert have distinct codes, following the same convention the table already uses (browser keyCode: F1 = 112 ... F12 = 123, F10 = 121 already matches; Insert = 45).\n\nSuggested change: add Key::F1..Key::F9, Key::F11, Key::F12 (112-120, 122, 123) and Key::Insert (45) to the match. We are happy to send a pull request.\n\nNot claimed: the numeric keypad. With NumLock on, winit reports the keypad digits as characters, so they arrive as Key::Num0..Key::Num9 and get the digit-row codes (48-57); the keypad cannot be told apart from the main block, but we did not see code 0 for it in the source.\n\nReference in our fork: git-bug issue (id given in the cover message).","files":null}]}